{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc 'cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/report.md\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/analysis/group_analysis.py\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/input/upstream-methods.md\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/results/summary.json\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/results/input_qc.csv\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/results/fwe_clusters.csv\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/results/vmPFC_participant_effects.csv\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/results/vmPFC_mask_definition.txt\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/input/participants.tsv\" | head -15; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/input/sources.json\"'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc 'cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/report.md\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/analysis/group_analysis.py\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/input/upstream-methods.md\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/results/summary.json\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/results/input_qc.csv\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/results/fwe_clusters.csv\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/results/vmPFC_participant_effects.csv\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/results/vmPFC_mask_definition.txt\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/input/participants.tsv\" | head -15; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/input/sources.json\"'","aggregated_output":"# Potential gains and brain activity in the equal-indifference group\n\n## Result\n\n**This group analysis did not support the predicted positive gain effect in vmPFC.** Among 54 equal-indifference participants, no voxel in the predefined vmPFC localization mask survived one-sided whole-brain family-wise error (FWE) correction (10,000 participant-level sign flips; max-statistic correction; α = .05). There were 24 FWE-significant positive voxels elsewhere, in three small posterior occipital clusters; these do not support the vmPFC hypothesis. The strongest voxel was at MNI (−26, −102, −3.6 mm), mean effect estimate 0.112, t(53) = 6.62, max-T FWE p = 0.00060.\n\nThe descriptive average across the operational vmPFC mask was −0.00051 estimate units (95% t interval −0.0222 to 0.0212; one-sided uncorrected p = .519; standardized one-sample effect d = −0.006). The interval includes positive and negative effects. Thus the result is **lack of corrected evidence for the predicted increase**, not proof that the true vmPFC effect is zero or evidence against every plausible positive effect.\n\n![Mean gain effect and whole-brain corrected discoveries](figures/whole_brain_mean_and_fwe.png)\n\n*Figure 1. Mean participant gain-effect estimates across the common brain coverage mask. Black contours mark voxels surviving positive one-sided max-T whole-brain FWE correction at .05. The surviving posterior voxels are far from vmPFC.*\n\n![Participant vmPFC estimates](figures/vmPFC_participant_effects.png)\n\n*Figure 2. Each point is one participant’s average effect estimate within the vmPFC mask. The band shows the uncorrected t-based 95% interval for the mean of those 54 participant summaries; it is descriptive and does not replace the whole-brain-corrected test.*\n\n## Analysis\n\nThe original NARPS instructions specify the directional hypothesis as a positive parametric gain effect in vmPFC for the equal-indifference group and ask teams to report whole-brain-corrected results ([instructions, Hypothesis 1](https://www.narps.info/files/narps_instructions_for_analysis_teams.pdf)). vmPFC does not have one universally agreed anatomical boundary; the NARPS report describes using a conjunction of anatomical and meta-analytic information to operationalize it ([NARPS study](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41586-020-2314-9)). Here, the primary support criterion is a positive whole-brain-corrected voxel in an independently specified vmPFC mask. For transparent localization, the mask uses Harvard–Oxford cortical atlas labels for Frontal Medial Cortex restricted to z ≤ 30 mm, Subcallosal Cortex, and the Frontal Orbital label restricted to |MNI x| ≤ 20 mm and z ≤ 30 mm ([Nilearn atlas documentation](https://nilearn.github.io/stable/modules/generated/nilearn.datasets.fetch_atlas_harvard_oxford.html)). The atlas uses a different MNI template from the participant maps; labels are resampled to the participant-map grid with nearest-neighbour interpolation and intersected with common image coverage. This is an operational ROI, not a uniquely established boundary, and atlas-template differences add spatial uncertainty.\n\nFor each voxel, we estimated the group mean of the 54 participant effect maps and calculated a one-sample t statistic. Positive one-sided inference matches the preregistered directional hypothesis. Family-wise correction used the distribution of the maximum t statistic over all 198,029 voxels in the common map coverage mask under 10,000 randomly generated participant-level sign flips (seed 20261016). Each flip was applied to a participant’s entire 3D map, preserving within-map spatial dependence. The 95th percentile of the maximum-t null was 5.381. The atlas ROI average is summarized by participant before computing its interval; voxels are not treated as independent observations.\n\n## Inputs, quality checks, and limits\n\nAll 54 supplied participant maps matched the byte count and SHA-256 checksum in `input/sources.json`; all had the expected 97 × 115 × 81 geometry, matching affines and finite data. The common nonzero coverage comprised 198,029 voxels (about 1,901 ml at the map’s 2 × 2 × 2.4 mm resolution). No participant was excluded. Metadata were consistent with the supplied cohort: mean age 26.1 years (SD 3.0), 30 women and 24 men. Input-level checks are in [`results/input_qc.csv`](results/input_qc.csv).\n\nThese are participant contrast parameter estimates, not standardized test statistics. As described in [`input/upstream-methods.md`](input/upstream-methods.md), the gain modulator was mean-centered and variance-normalized before HRF convolution, so map values are not change per currency unit. The maps represent the gain coefficient conditional on the upstream model’s other regressors and separate gain/loss modeling choices. Group differences here cannot be converted into a response per dollar, and the upstream choices cannot be re-evaluated from these images alone. Participant variance maps and the executable individual-level pipeline were not supplied.\n\nThe sign-flip test assumes independent participants and a symmetric null distribution of participant-level effects. The finite permutation sample gives approximate Monte Carlo p-values; the anatomical vmPFC definition and the source maps’ fixed model choices also constrain interpretation. A nonsignificant corrected result means these data and this analysis did not provide sufficiently strong, spatially localized whole-brain evidence for the specified effect. It does not establish absence, rule out smaller effects, or generalize to other gain ranges, groups, or model specifications. The study’s equal-indifference task used a potential-gain range twice the potential-loss range.\n\n## Reproducibility files\n\n- [`analysis/group_analysis.py`](analysis/group_analysis.py): complete group analysis, input integrity checks, atlas ROI construction, permutation inference and figure generation.\n- [`results/summary.json`](results/summary.json): principal numeric results and analysis settings.\n- [`results/fwe_clusters.csv`](results/fwe_clusters.csv), [`results/top_voxels.csv`](results/top_voxels.csv): significant cluster and peak summaries.\n- [`results/vmPFC_participant_effects.csv`](results/vmPFC_participant_effects.csv): independent participant summaries underlying Figure 2.\n- [`results/vmPFC_mask_definition.txt`](results/vmPFC_mask_definition.txt): ROI recipe.\n- NIfTI outputs in `results/`: group mean, t map, max-T FWE p map, significant-voxel map and vmPFC mask.\n\nThe analysis used Python 3.13.16, NumPy 2.5.4, SciPy 1.18.1, pandas 3.0.6, NiBabel 5.4.2 and Nilearn 0.14.1. The available skill catalog was checked; it contained no neuroimaging/group-analysis skill applicable to this workflow, so no unrelated skill was installed.\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Group analysis of NARPS AO86 participant-level gain-effect estimates.\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport hashlib\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport nibabel as nib\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nfrom nilearn import datasets, image, plotting\nfrom scipy.stats import t as t_dist\nfrom scipy.ndimage import label\n\n\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]\nINPUT = ROOT / \"input\"\nOUT = ROOT / \"results\"\nFIG = ROOT / \"figures\"\nOUT.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\nFIG.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\nN_PERM = 10000\nSEED = 20261016\n\n\ndef sha256(path: Path) -> str:\n    h = hashlib.sha256()\n    with path.open(\"rb\") as f:\n        for block in iter(lambda: f.read(1024 * 1024), b\"\"):\n            h.update(block)\n    return h.hexdigest()\n\n\ndef save_map(data: np.ndarray, ref: nib.spatialimages.SpatialImage, path: Path) -> None:\n    hdr = ref.header.copy()\n    hdr.set_data_dtype(np.float32)\n    nib.save(nib.Nifti1Image(data.astype(np.float32), ref.affine, hdr), path)\n\n\ndef main() -> None:\n    meta = pd.read_csv(INPUT / \"participants.tsv\", sep=\"\\t\")\n    manifest = json.loads((INPUT / \"sources.json\").read_text())\n    ids = meta.participant_id.tolist()\n    paths = [INPUT / \"data\" / f\"{pid}_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\" for pid in ids]\n    qc = []\n    imgs = []\n    for pid, path in zip(ids, paths):\n        if not path.is_file():\n            raise FileNotFoundError(path)\n        img = nib.load(path)\n        expected = manifest[\"files\"][f\"data/{path.name}\"]\n        digest = sha256(path)\n        qc.append({\"participant_id\": pid, \"bytes\": path.stat().st_size,\n                   \"expected_bytes\": expected[\"bytes\"], \"sha256\": digest,\n                   \"expected_sha256\": expected[\"sha256\"],\n                   \"shape\": \"x\".join(map(str, img.shape)),\n                   \"finite_fraction\": float(np.isfinite(np.asarray(img.dataobj)).mean()),\n                   \"nonzero_voxels\": int(np.count_nonzero(np.asarray(img.dataobj)))})\n        if path.stat().st_size != expected[\"bytes\"] or digest != expected[\"sha256\"]:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Input integrity check failed: {path}\")\n        if img.shape != (97, 115, 81):\n            raise ValueError(f\"Unexpected shape for {path}: {img.shape}\")\n        imgs.append(img)\n    pd.DataFrame(qc).to_csv(OUT / \"input_qc.csv\", index=False)\n\n    ref = imgs[0]\n    arr = np.stack([np.asarray(im.dataobj, dtype=np.float32) for im in imgs])\n    if not all(np.allclose(im.affine, ref.affine) for im in imgs):\n        raise ValueError(\"Participant images have inconsistent affines\")\n    if not np.isfinite(arr).all():\n        raise ValueError(\"Non-finite participant effect values\")\n    # The inputs share a brain coverage mask. Use its intersection for all tests.\n    mask = np.all(arr != 0, axis=0)\n    n = arr.shape[0]\n    flat = arr[:, mask].astype(np.float64, copy=False)\n    m = flat.mean(axis=0)\n    ss = np.einsum(\"ij,ij->j\", flat, flat)\n    sd = flat.std(axis=0, ddof=1)\n    t_obs = m / (sd / np.sqrt(n))\n    p_unc = t_dist.sf(t_obs, df=n - 1)\n\n    # One-sided max-t sign-flip permutation: participant maps are independent\n    # units; one sign is applied to each full image on every draw.\n    rng = np.random.default_rng(SEED)\n    max_t = np.empty(N_PERM, dtype=np.float32)\n    chunk = 128\n    denom_const = float(n - 1)\n    for start in range(0, N_PERM, chunk):\n        k = min(chunk, N_PERM - start)\n        signs = rng.choice(np.array([-1.0, 1.0]), size=(k, n))\n        sums = signs @ flat\n        denom_sq = (n * ss[None, :] - sums * sums) / denom_const\n        t_perm = sums / np.sqrt(np.maximum(denom_sq, 1e-30))\n        max_t[start:start + k] = np.max(t_perm, axis=1)\n    max_t.sort()\n    p_fwe = (1 + N_PERM - np.searchsorted(max_t, t_obs, side=\"left\")) / (N_PERM + 1)\n    threshold = float(max_t[int(np.ceil((1 - 0.05) * (N_PERM + 1))) - 1])\n\n    mean_img = np.zeros(ref.shape, np.float32)\n    t_img = np.zeros(ref.shape, np.float32)\n    p_img = np.ones(ref.shape, np.float32)\n    fwe_img = np.zeros(ref.shape, np.uint8)\n    mean_img[mask] = m\n    t_img[mask] = t_obs\n    p_img[mask] = p_fwe\n    fwe_img[mask] = p_fwe < 0.05\n    save_map(mean_img, ref, OUT / \"group_mean_effect.nii.gz\")\n    save_map(t_img, ref, OUT / \"group_t.nii.gz\")\n    save_map(p_img, ref, OUT / \"p_fwe_maxT.nii.gz\")\n    nib.save(nib.Nifti1Image(fwe_img, ref.affine, ref.header), OUT / \"significant_fwe.nii.gz\")\n\n    # A transparent anatomical operationalization of vmPFC for localization.\n    # Harvard-Oxford cortical labels (Frontal Medial, Subcallosal, and the\n    # medial portion of Frontal Orbital), resampled nearest-neighbour to data.\n    ho = datasets.fetch_atlas_harvard_oxford(\"cort-maxprob-thr25-2mm\")\n    atlas_img = image.resample_to_img(ho.maps, ref, interpolation=\"nearest\")\n    atlas = np.asarray(atlas_img.dataobj)\n    # Labels are 25=Frontal Medial, 27=Subcallosal, 33=Frontal Orbital.\n    ijk = np.indices(ref.shape)\n    xyz = nib.affines.apply_affine(ref.affine, np.stack(ijk, axis=-1).reshape(-1, 3)).reshape((*ref.shape, 3))\n    roi = (((atlas == 25) & (xyz[..., 2] <= 30)) | (atlas == 27) |\n           ((atlas == 33) & (np.abs(xyz[..., 0]) <= 20) & (xyz[..., 2] <= 30)))\n    roi &= mask\n    save_map(roi.astype(np.float32), ref, OUT / \"vmPFC_operational_mask.nii.gz\")\n    roi_n = int(roi.sum())\n    roi_vals = arr[:, roi]\n    subj_roi = roi_vals.mean(axis=1)\n    roi_mean = float(subj_roi.mean())\n    roi_sd = float(subj_roi.std(ddof=1))\n    roi_se = roi_sd / np.sqrt(n)\n    roi_t = roi_mean / roi_se\n    roi_p = float(t_dist.sf(roi_t, n - 1))\n    roi_ci = [float(roi_mean - t_dist.ppf(.975, n - 1) * roi_se),\n              float(roi_mean + t_dist.ppf(.975, n - 1) * roi_se)]\n    roi_d = roi_mean / roi_sd\n    sig_idx = np.flatnonzero((p_fwe < .05))\n    voxel_idx = np.flatnonzero(mask)\n    sig_voxels = voxel_idx[sig_idx]\n    sig_coords = nib.affines.apply_affine(ref.affine, np.array(np.unravel_index(sig_voxels, ref.shape)).T) if len(sig_voxels) else np.empty((0, 3))\n    roi_sig_count = int(np.sum((p_fwe < .05) & roi[mask]))\n    pd.DataFrame({\"participant_id\": ids, \"vmPFC_mean_effect\": subj_roi}).to_csv(\n        OUT / \"vmPFC_participant_effects.csv\", index=False)\n\n    # Peak coordinates and effect sizes for the most significant positive voxels.\n    top = np.argsort(-t_obs)[:10]\n    top_vox = voxel_idx[top]\n    top_xyz = nib.affines.apply_affine(ref.affine, np.array(np.unravel_index(top_vox, ref.shape)).T)\n    peaks = []\n    for j, xyzj in zip(top, top_xyz):\n        peaks.append({\"x\": float(xyzj[0]), \"y\": float(xyzj[1]), \"z\": float(xyzj[2]),\n                      \"mean_effect\": float(m[j]), \"t\": float(t_obs[j]),\n                      \"p_uncorrected\": float(p_unc[j]), \"p_fwe_maxT\": float(p_fwe[j]),\n                      \"vmPFC_operational_roi\": bool(roi[tuple(np.unravel_index(voxel_idx[j], ref.shape))])})\n    pd.DataFrame(peaks).to_csv(OUT / \"top_voxels.csv\", index=False)\n\n    # Cluster summaries describe, but do not replace, voxelwise max-T inference.\n    labels, n_labels = label(fwe_img, structure=np.ones((3, 3, 3), dtype=np.uint8))\n    clusters = []\n    for lab in range(1, n_labels + 1):\n        vox = np.flatnonzero(labels == lab)\n        flat_indices = np.ravel_multi_index(np.array(np.unravel_index(vox, ref.shape)), ref.shape)\n        mask_positions = np.searchsorted(voxel_idx, flat_indices)\n        best = mask_positions[np.argmax(t_obs[mask_positions])]\n        peak_ijk = np.unravel_index(voxel_idx[best], ref.shape)\n        peak_xyz = nib.affines.apply_affine(ref.affine, peak_ijk)\n        clusters.append({\"cluster\": lab, \"voxels_26_connected\": int(len(vox)),\n                         \"volume_mm3\": float(len(vox) * np.prod(ref.header.get_zooms()[:3])),\n                         \"peak_x\": float(peak_xyz[0]), \"peak_y\": float(peak_xyz[1]),\n                         \"peak_z\": float(peak_xyz[2]), \"peak_t\": float(t_obs[best]),\n                         \"peak_mean_effect\": float(m[best]),\n                         \"peak_p_fwe_maxT\": float(p_fwe[best])})\n    pd.DataFrame(clusters).to_csv(OUT / \"fwe_clusters.csv\", index=False)\n\n    result = {\n        \"n_participants\": n, \"excluded_participants\": [], \"mask_voxels\": int(mask.sum()),\n        \"voxel_volume_mm3\": float(np.prod(ref.header.get_zooms()[:3])),\n        \"whole_brain_mask_volume_ml\": float(mask.sum() * np.prod(ref.header.get_zooms()[:3]) / 1000),\n        \"permutations\": N_PERM, \"seed\": SEED, \"one_sided\": True,\n        \"maxT_95_threshold\": threshold, \"whole_brain_fwe_significant_voxels\": int((p_fwe < .05).sum()),\n        \"vmPFC_mask_voxels\": roi_n, \"vmPFC_mask_volume_ml\": float(roi_n * np.prod(ref.header.get_zooms()[:3]) / 1000),\n        \"vmPFC_mean_effect\": roi_mean, \"vmPFC_95ci_t\": roi_ci, \"vmPFC_dz\": roi_d,\n        \"vmPFC_t\": float(roi_t), \"vmPFC_p_one_sided_uncorrected\": roi_p,\n        \"vmPFC_fwe_significant_voxels\": roi_sig_count,\n        \"top_voxel\": peaks[0],\n        \"group_mean_effect_median_in_mask\": float(np.median(m)),\n        \"participant_map_mean_median\": float(np.median(arr[:, mask].mean(axis=1))),\n        \"participant_map_mean_range\": [float(arr[:, mask].mean(axis=1).min()), float(arr[:, mask].mean(axis=1).max())],\n        \"age_mean\": float(meta.age.mean()), \"age_sd\": float(meta.age.std(ddof=1)),\n        \"gender_counts\": meta.gender.value_counts().sort_index().to_dict(),\n    }\n    (OUT / \"summary.json\").write_text(json.dumps(result, indent=2))\n\n    # Figure 1: whole-brain continuous average map and FWE discoveries.\n    mean_nii = nib.load(OUT / \"group_mean_effect.nii.gz\")\n    sig_nii = nib.load(OUT / \"significant_fwe.nii.gz\")\n    fig = plt.figure(figsize=(12, 4.2))\n    display = plotting.plot_stat_map(mean_nii, display_mode=\"z\", cut_coords=[-8, 0, 8, 16, 24, 32],\n                                    threshold=0, cmap=\"coolwarm\", symmetric_cbar=True,\n                                    title=\"Mean gain-effect estimate (unthresholded; FWE discoveries outlined)\",\n                                    colorbar=True, figure=fig, vmax=float(np.percentile(np.abs(m), 99.5)))\n    display.add_contours(sig_nii, levels=[.5], colors=\"black\", linewidths=1.5)\n    display.savefig(FIG / \"whole_brain_mean_and_fwe.png\", dpi=180)\n    plt.close(fig)\n\n    # Figure 2: independent unit-level ROI summary, no voxel pseudoreplication.\n    fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7.4, 4.6))\n    rng_plot = np.random.default_rng(SEED)\n    ax.scatter(rng_plot.normal(0, .045, n), subj_roi, s=28, alpha=.72, color=\"#355C7D\", edgecolor=\"white\", linewidth=.35)\n    ax.axhline(0, color=\"0.4\", lw=1, ls=\"--\")\n    ax.axhline(roi_mean, color=\"#C06C84\", lw=2, label=f\"mean = {roi_mean:.3g}\")\n    ax.fill_between([-.14, .14], roi_ci[0], roi_ci[1], color=\"#C06C84\", alpha=.16,\n                    label=f\"95% CI [{roi_ci[0]:.3g}, {roi_ci[1]:.3g}]\")\n    ax.set(xlim=(-.16, .16), xlabel=\"Participants (jittered)\", ylabel=\"Mean gain-effect estimate in vmPFC mask\",\n           title=\"Participant-level vmPFC estimates (n=54)\")\n    ax.set_xticks([])\n    ax.legend(frameon=False, loc=\"best\")\n    fig.tight_layout()\n    fig.savefig(FIG / \"vmPFC_participant_effects.png\", dpi=180)\n    plt.close(fig)\n\n    # Save atlas label rationale and exact mask definition for reproducibility.\n    (OUT / \"vmPFC_mask_definition.txt\").write_text(\n        \"Harvard-Oxford cortical max-probability atlas, threshold 25%, labels: \"\n        \"Frontal Medial Cortex (25) restricted to z <= 30 mm, Subcallosal Cortex (27), and \"\n        \"Frontal Orbital Cortex (33) restricted to |MNI x| <= 20 mm and z <= 30 mm \"\n        \"restricted to |MNI x| <= 20 mm and z <= 30 mm; nearest-neighbour resampled to participant grid; \"\n        \"intersected with common participant nonzero coverage. This is an operational anatomical localization mask, \"\n        \"not a uniquely established vmPFC boundary. Whole-brain max-T inference is primary.\\n\"\n    )\n    print(json.dumps(result, indent=2))\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    main()\n# Fixed upstream analysis\n\nEach map is one participant's unthresholded gain-effect estimate from NARPS team AO86, averaged across four runs.\nThe 54 participants comprise the complete equal-indifference group in OpenNeuro ds001734 version 1.0.4.\nThese are effect estimates, not standardized test statistics or group results.\nMaps are reported in MNI152NLin2009cAsym space.\n\nAO86 used fMRIPrep outputs and nistats participant models.\nTheir reported gain regressor was mean-centered and variance-normalized before HRF convolution; its coefficients must not be interpreted as change per currency unit.\nResponse trials used response-time durations, with separate response-choice and gain modulators.\nNonresponse trials, motion terms, flagged volumes and drift terms were also modeled; runs were combined within participant.\nGain and loss used separate models, so the supplied gain effect is conditional on that modeling choice.\n\nThe reported upstream choices are fixed for this task.\nParticipant variance maps and a complete executable upstream pipeline are not supplied; account for these limits when choosing and interpreting group inference.\n`participants.tsv` retains the source group, age and gender columns.\n`sources.json` identifies each original image, its retrieved-byte checksum, the pinned cohort table and the original methods report.\n{\n  \"n_participants\": 54,\n  \"excluded_participants\": [],\n  \"mask_voxels\": 198029,\n  \"voxel_volume_mm3\": 9.600000381469727,\n  \"whole_brain_mask_volume_ml\": 1901.0784755420684,\n  \"permutations\": 10000,\n  \"seed\": 20261016,\n  \"one_sided\": true,\n  \"maxT_95_threshold\": 5.381128311157227,\n  \"whole_brain_fwe_significant_voxels\": 24,\n  \"vmPFC_mask_voxels\": 1821,\n  \"vmPFC_mask_volume_ml\": 17.48160171508789,\n  \"vmPFC_mean_effect\": -0.0005117690889164805,\n  \"vmPFC_95ci_t\": [\n    -0.02221120104246767,\n    0.02118766286463471\n  ],\n  \"vmPFC_dz\": -0.006437314679453736,\n  \"vmPFC_t\": -0.047304408835168524,\n  \"vmPFC_p_one_sided_uncorrected\": 0.5187757912614934,\n  \"vmPFC_fwe_significant_voxels\": 0,\n  \"top_voxel\": {\n    \"x\": -26.0,\n    \"y\": -102.0,\n    \"z\": -3.599997043609619,\n    \"mean_effect\": 0.11212667576209814,\n    \"t\": 6.617173246302241,\n    \"p_uncorrected\": 9.311564027968496e-09,\n    \"p_fwe_maxT\": 0.0005999400059994001,\n    \"vmPFC_operational_roi\": false\n  },\n  \"group_mean_effect_median_in_mask\": -0.00668044062115735,\n  \"participant_map_mean_median\": -0.008956247940659523,\n  \"participant_map_mean_range\": [\n    -0.1336822509765625,\n    0.10376109182834625\n  ],\n  \"age_mean\": 26.055555555555557,\n  \"age_sd\": 3.0493838948818466,\n  \"gender_counts\": {\n    \"F\": 30,\n    \"M\": 24\n  }\n}participant_id,bytes,expected_bytes,sha256,expected_sha256,shape,finite_fraction,nonzero_voxels\nsub-001,1794522,1794522,baf3440fbc7f5f59dea28d231e9c5eb9cf00a15b6924342f4b79e7faf56a929a,baf3440fbc7f5f59dea28d231e9c5eb9cf00a15b6924342f4b79e7faf56a929a,97x115x81,1.0,225441\nsub-003,1762018,1762018,a8c9b6276d1597bc43695516c9ac5b2893a8d8408cd053161ceaddbd42dafb20,a8c9b6276d1597bc43695516c9ac5b2893a8d8408cd053161ceaddbd42dafb20,97x115x81,1.0,221352\nsub-005,1847315,1847315,415383874c65fd9e10bb03bf2aa59bfcd1d10650bc5d0560a249e85d52b7b205,415383874c65fd9e10bb03bf2aa59bfcd1d10650bc5d0560a249e85d52b7b205,97x115x81,1.0,232390\nsub-009,1737182,1737182,33af129d8ba13cd4b6175089706c531471be1e0bd7ccd43ece10751c8cc3254c,33af129d8ba13cd4b6175089706c531471be1e0bd7ccd43ece10751c8cc3254c,97x115x81,1.0,218048\nsub-011,1828116,1828116,6f74dd449eefa6eff40ec00eb046ec7e8bb13c0b7c2ac1874155a9c166692352,6f74dd449eefa6eff40ec00eb046ec7e8bb13c0b7c2ac1874155a9c166692352,97x115x81,1.0,229790\nsub-013,1760573,1760573,bfa79b82f931d81d6387e82b8413f1f889a06f36275ba510554669c84a3c026f,bfa79b82f931d81d6387e82b8413f1f889a06f36275ba510554669c84a3c026f,97x115x81,1.0,221025\nsub-015,1805181,1805181,5d98f6d74823516768659967f95ef43728584eab024c58af30987783515d7694,5d98f6d74823516768659967f95ef43728584eab024c58af30987783515d7694,97x115x81,1.0,226823\nsub-017,1791857,1791857,dca9eac4d6f0ef4e2a3e631b2d10f89c5362863698ce