e-format.md\n/Users/johnlee/.local/share/wt-utils/worktrees/73e0/stamped-in-practice/scripts/../assessment/skills/stamped-conventions/references/project-kinds.md\n/Users/johnlee/.local/share/wt-utils/worktrees/73e0/stamped-in-practice/scripts/../assessment/skills/stamped-conventions/references/verdicts.md\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc 'cat \"$(dirname \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\")/../assessment/skills/stamped-conventions/references/response-format.md\"; cat \"$(dirname \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\")/../assessment/skills/stamped-conventions/references/verdicts.md\"; cat \"$(dirname \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\")/../protocols/neutral-v1/requirements.json\"'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc 'cat \"$(dirname \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\")/../assessment/skills/stamped-conventions/references/response-format.md\"; cat \"$(dirname \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\")/../assessment/skills/stamped-conventions/references/verdicts.md\"; cat \"$(dirname \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\")/../protocols/neutral-v1/requirements.json\"'","aggregated_output":"# Response, report and stamp\n\nEvery STAMPED assessment produces four files (five with the optional fresh-rerun report), named for **the day the assessment was made** (UTC, `YYYY-MM-DD`) — not the object's commit date, and not the date of an earlier assessment being redone:\n\n| File | What it is |\n|---|---|\n| `<date>-assessment.md` | the human report (layout below) |\n| `<date>-response.json` | the machine-readable per-item answer, with evidence and pins |\n| `<date>-probes.json` | what the scripted probes said, unedited, including the ones overridden |\n| `<date>-url.txt` | the checklist-app URL, on one line |\n| `<date>-fresh-rerun.json` | only when the expensive rerun probe was actually run |\n\nThe URL is derived from the response, never maintained by hand.\nTwo assessments of the same object on the same day get a `-2` suffix on all four; the second one's report says what changed and why.\nWhere the files are written is the calling skill's business; their names and shapes are this file's.\n\n## Response JSON\n\nA superset of the web app's persistence format 3.\nThe app ignores keys it does not know, so one file serves both.\n\n```json\n{\n  \"checklist_version\": \"0.3.1\",\n  \"responses\": {\n    \"stamped-checklist:must/009\": {\n      \"value\": \"no\",\n      \"reason\": \"code/plot.py line 12 opens an absolute path under a user home directory.\",\n      \"status\": \"unmet\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\"uri\": \"code/plot.py#L12\", \"description\": \"open(<absolute path under $HOME>)\", \"sha256\": null}\n      ],\n      \"source\": \"probe:no-abs-paths\",\n      \"flags\": []\n    }\n  },\n  \"schema_version\": \"0.2.0\",\n  \"principles_version\": \"0.2.0\",\n  \"checklist_source\": {\n    \"repo\": \"https://github.com/stamped-principles/stamped-checklist-schema\",\n    \"tag\": \"v0.3.1\",\n    \"commit\": \"af259bad237fa57b856417db8dba45a1b283a527\"\n  },\n  \"extra_items\": [\n    {\"key\": \"licence-declared\", \"status\": \"unmet\", \"reason\": \"No LICENSE, LICENSES/ or REUSE.toml; paper S.2/D.3 have no checklist item.\", \"evidence\": []}\n  ],\n  \"object\": {\"url\": \"…\", \"commit\": \"…\", \"captured_at\": \"2026-10-13T12:00:00Z\", \"kind\": \"study\"},\n  \"assessor\": {\"skill\": \"assess-alignment-with-stamped-principles\", \"version\": \"0.2.0\", \"harness\": \"claude-code 2.1.x\", \"model\": \"…\", \"date\": \"2026-10-13\",\n               \"dependencies\": [\"stamped-conventions@0.2.0\"]},\n  \"scope\": \"Assessed the repository at the commit above; container images were not pulled.\"\n}\n```\n\n- **Required core** (what the app reads): `checklist_version`, and `responses` with a `value` and a string `reason` per id — *in the payload the encoder builds*. The response JSON you write carries `status`, and `encode` derives `value` from it; `validate` therefore does not ask for a `value` and `set` does not write one. A `value` you write by hand that disagrees with its `status` is an error `validate` reports.\n- Every one of the 31 pinned ids appears exactly once.\n  A missing id is not \"no answer\"; it is a bug the validator catches.\n- `status` is the richer verdict from [`verdicts.md`](verdicts.md); `value` is derived from it, never set by hand.\n- **Cite a line only if you opened it.** `path#L12` is a promise that line 12 says what the reason claims; a run that cited line numbers it had not read then had to strip them all. Without that, cite the file (or `path#section`), which is still a citation a reader can follow.\n- `evidence[]` uses stamped-skills-assessment's `Evidence` shape: `{uri, description, sha256?}`.\n  `uri` is relative to the object's root, or absolute for anything outside it.\n  **The argument lives here, not in `reason`.** 250 characters does not hold two cited accounts plus \"the probe's heuristic said X but Y is true\", and an override needs all three. Make `reason` the one-line verdict a reader sees in the app, and put each account, each command you ran and each number in an `evidence[]` entry with its own `description`.\n- `source` is `probe:<name>`, `judgement`, or `probe:<name>+judgement` when a reading of the object overrode a probe (and then the item is flagged).\n  Several probes may be named — `probe:entry-point+probe:ci-present+judgement` — which is what a verdict reached by **comparing two probes' evidence** looks like, and that comparison is itself the finding often enough to be worth recording.\n- `flags[]` is optional and holds what a reader has to know about *how* the answer was reached, not what it says:\n  - `mapping-questioned` — the probe answered this item from a property the item does not ask about ([`verdicts.md`](verdicts.md) rule 10);\n  - `probe-overridden` — a reading of the object changed the probe's verdict;\n  - `unverified-claim` — the verdict rests on something not confirmed independently, and the report says so;\n  - `upstream-duplicate` — the checklist asks this question twice (must/014 and should/008 are the same sentence), so this answer repeats another by design rather than by mistake.\n\n  Flagged items get their own short list in the report, after the per-item table.\n  The list is **open**: these four are the ones in use, and a consumer that meets an unfamiliar flag shows it rather than dropping it.\n- The `assessor` block may also carry `drafted_by` (the script or step that produced a draft, where that differs from the skill — `scripts/run_probes.py` sets it) and `note` (anything a reader needs about the assessor itself rather than about the object). Both are provenance *within* the assessor, not a second assessor.\n- `extra_items[]` carries findings with no checklist id (licences, T.4) so they are reported without bending an existing id.\n- `object.commit` names the inspected repository or preservation revision, not necessarily the worker's delivered state.\n  Identify the original delivery snapshot/hash, capture omissions and stop-state evidence in `scope` and evidence citations; distinguish supplied resources and coordinator additions so they cannot earn worker credit. Keep the actual Git commit whenever one exists. For a delivery without a commit, set `object.commit` to `null` and supply `object.capture` with a retained manifest/archive's `uri` and SHA-256 `sha256`; never substitute the capture hash for a Git commit.\n  The manifest must identify the delivered paths and content hashes plus capture omissions; an archive must contain the assessed bytes.\n  Retain the referenced file so another assessor can verify the hash and boundary.\n  Use `encode_response.py template -o <date>-response.json` when no Git probe draft is available, fill the assessment metadata and answers, then run `encode_response.py stamp <date>-response.json --capture <manifest-or-archive>` to hash the actual file.\n  This preserves an existing commit; record the capture time in `object.captured_at` and the boundary in `scope`.\n  Validation checks the record's shape; independently verify the manifest against the delivery.\n\n`validate` checks the complete record against [`response-schema.json`](response-schema.json), using `jsonschema`, then checks checklist pins and verdict semantics. Stamp the response before final validation. The metadata is required for a completed assessment, even though the web app itself reads only the compact payload.\n\n## The provenance stamp\n\n`assessor` plus `object` plus `checklist_source` **is** the stamp, and every report repeats it in prose at its foot:\n\n> Produced by `assess-alignment-with-stamped-principles` 0.1.0 with `stamped-conventions` 0.1.0, checklist 0.3.1 (`stamped-checklist-schema@v0.3.1`, `af259ba`), on <object url> at commit `<sha>`, 2026-10-13, <harness>/<model>.\n> Probe output: `<path to the probe JSON>`.\n\nWhen no commit exists, replace `at commit <sha>` with `at capture <uri>, SHA-256 <sha256>` in the prose stamp.\n\nA report without the stamp cannot be compared against another iteration, so it is not finished.\n\n## Report layout\n\nUse this order, so the reader meets the actionable part first:\n\n1. **Verdict line** — `MUST: n met / n partial / n unmet / n not-checked`, then SHOULD and MAY, each with its denominator.\n   No single overall score, ever.\n2. **What to fix first** — at most five, highest leverage first, each as *what to change* → *why it matters to this project* → *the item ids it closes*.\n3. **What the project already does well** — two or three, with citations.\n4. **Per-item table** — MUST first, then SHOULD, then MAY: id, principle codes, status, evidence, reason.\n   `scripts/encode_response.py table <response>.json` renders exactly this from the response; it is not a table to type by hand.\n5. **Flagged items** — every item carrying a `flags` entry, with one line each: what the probe measured versus what the item asks, or what the override was.\n   An empty list is worth stating as \"none\".\n6. **Not checked** — each with what would settle it.\n7. **Goals and kind** — the project's stated goals (and where they came from: documentation, or the user's answers), its kind, and the emphasis that follows.\n8. **The checklist URL**, the stamp, and a pointer to the response JSON.\n   The URL is ~8–10 kB of base64 for a full 31-item response, so the report **links** `<date>-url.txt` rather than inlining it; a link a reader can click, not a wall they have to scroll.\n\n## The checklist-app URL\n\nFacts, from [stamped-checklist](https://github.com/stamped-principles/stamped-checklist) at `d24d7ca`:\n\n- `PERSISTENCE_FORMAT = 3` (`src/persistence.js`).\n- The `responses` parameter is **standard base64** (not URL-safe) of UTF-8 JSON `{\"checklist_version\", \"responses\"}`, then percent-encoded as a query parameter.\n- The app builds `?checklist=<v>&responses_version=<v>&cols=auto&sections=off&format=3&responses=<b64>` (`src/script.js`, `syncPersistentURL`).\n- It accepts `value ∈ {\"yes\",\"no\",null}`, requires `reason` to be a **string** (empty is fine), truncates it to 250 characters, shows it only for `\"no\"`, drops keys it does not know, and **rejects the whole load** on an id that is not in the requested checklist version.\n\n`scripts/encode_response.py` is the only writer of that URL, and `decode` is the reverse — that is how a checklist a person filled in the web app becomes reference answers for calibration, with no extra labelling tool.\n\n## When the full assessment record matters\n\nThe response JSON is the canonical assessment record. When a calling evaluation harness supplies pinned export schemas, derive its additional records from that JSON; do not invent formats or make an absent exporter block the assessment.\n# Verdict vocabulary\n\nOne status per checklist item, one reason, and evidence for anything other than `not-applicable`.\nThis reconciles @asmacdo's draft (pass/partial/fail/unknown), STAMPED-dl's met/partial/unmet/restricted/n-a, the [K-Dense #265](https://github.com/K-Dense-AI/scientific-agent-skills/pull/265) review (\"not checked with the tooling available\" is not \"unmet\"), and the judgement statuses of [stamped-skills-assessment](https://github.com/stamped-principles/stamped-skills-assessment).\n\n| `status` | Use it when | App `value` | App `reason` holds |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `met` | Cited evidence shows the property holds across the object | `\"yes\"` | Not shown by the app; the citation lives in the response JSON |\n| `partial` | It holds by design but is violated somewhere specific, or holds in some components only | `\"no\"` | Where it falls short, concretely |\n| `unmet` | Evidence shows the property is absent | `\"no\"` | The shortest fix |\n| `not-checked` | You could not check: tool missing, cost, access, or out of the agreed scope | `null` | Why, and what would settle it |\n| `not-applicable` | The property cannot apply to this kind of object | `null` | `N/A: <reason>` |\n\n## Rules\n\n1. **A MUST item is never satisfied by `partial`.**\n   Report `partial` and count it as a MUST failure.\n2. **`not-checked` is reported separately from `unmet`, always.**\n   Never fold it in, never score it as zero, and never let it disappear from a denominator: say `14/17 MUST met, 2 unmet, 1 not-checked`.\n3. **`met` requires a citation** a reader can open: a path (with a line number where it helps), a commit, a run record, a URL, or a command with its output.\n   \"The project uses DataLad\" is not evidence; `.datalad/config` at commit `abc1234` is.\n4. **`unmet` requires evidence of absence**, not failure to find evidence.\n   If you only failed to find it, that is `not-checked`.\n5. **`not-applicable` is rare and argued**, and there are exactly two grounds for it:\n   - **the kind of object** ([`project-kinds.md`](project-kinds.md)) — an object with no computation at all can be `not-applicable` on E.1; a dataset still has an environment for whatever produced it;\n   - **an empty subject** — the thing the item asks about does not exist here.\n     should/009 (\"are restricted data or software kept in their own modules?\") on an object whose every component is public has no subject: `met` would be a citation-free claim about a component that does not exist, and `unmet` would be false.\n\n   Either way the reason names the ground, starting with `N/A:`.\n   An item you merely could not check is `not-checked`, never this.\n   After inspecting the complete delivery, use `not-applicable` for must/005 when no large binary data exists; apply the may/001 subject boundary below.\n\n   **Not** an N/A ground: *there was nothing of this kind to do*.\n   An object with no host assumptions to document satisfies must/011 — the requirement is \"write down your assumptions\", and having none meets it.\n   Answer `met`, and let the reason carry the emptiness (\"no host assumptions beyond a POSIX shell and the tracked lockfile; nothing undocumented remains\").\n   The difference from an empty subject is whether the *requirement* can be satisfied vacuously (must/011: yes) or the *property* has no instance to hold of (should/009 on an all-public object: no).\n\n   **A declared process that has never run is `partial`**, not `met` and not `not-checked`.\n   must/014 and should/008 ask for a process *and* for its updates to be tracked; a documented regeneration command with no update in the history is half of that, so say \"declared in <file>, no instance in the history yet\".\n   **The commit that introduced the file is not an instance of updating it.**\n   A lockfile committed once, with a regeneration command in its header, is \"declared, never run\": the creation is the declaration, not an instance of the process.\n   **A declared process with instances in the history is `met`**: cite one.\n   A blind run stalled on exactly this gap — a re-install step the history shows was carried out seventeen times fits neither \"never run\" nor \"unmet\" — so the three cases are: instances in the history → `met`; declared, no instance → `partial`; not declared → `unmet`.\n   **The bar for \"declared\" is a command a reader could run**: `# pinned with uv pip compile code/requirements.in` in a lockfile header clears it; \"dependencies are kept up to date\" does not, and that is `unmet`.\n   This is the commonest borderline case, and it comes up on every young object.\n6. **`restricted`** (from STAMPED-dl) is **not a status here**.\n   Restricted data that is properly isolated is `met` on should/009 and `not-checked` elsewhere with \"access-restricted\" as the reason.\n7. **Equivalent evidence counts.**\n   Name the mechanism you found rather than the tool you expected.\n   Preserve the checklist pin; these are explicit property-based interpretations, not edits to its wording:\n   - **must/003:** an installable package declaration in a delivered environment specification counts as inclusion; physical vendoring is not required. Judge completeness under must/010, exact environment identity under must/015, and archived artifacts under should/007; missing version pins alone do not show incompleteness.\n   - **must/007:** discoverable installation and usage instructions can satisfy the item under another filename or in an equivalent delivered interface; `README.md` and `Makefile` are not mandatory names.\n   - **may/001:** assess independently maintained project components, not ordinary runtime or package dependencies. No such components → `not-applicable`; otherwise require independently identified, retrievable links with clear boundaries, not necessarily Git submodules. An unidentified copy or unresolvable path fails. Judge missing or incomplete dependency environments under must/003 and must/010; they do not turn packages into project modules.\n   - **must/006:** execution evidence must link the actual results and actions to their environment; a recognized provenance format or tool name alone is neither necessary nor sufficient.\n   - **should/002, should/006:** inspect equivalent repeated-testing and environment-reconstitution records, including manual or scheduled runs; missing CI configuration alone proves neither property absent.\n   Apply these interpretations consistently within a comparison and name any equivalent evidence used.\n8. **Scripted before judged.**\n   Run applicable probes and cite their output, then inspect evidence outside their supported representations.\n   A tracked-path inventory, recognized filename or parsed DataLad record is not a complete delivered-artifact or provenance inventory.\n   Missing probe support is `not-checked` pending inspection, not evidence of absence; record which probe or reading produced each verdict.\n9. **Disagreements are surfaced, not averaged.**\n   When a probe and a reading of the object disagree, report both and flag the item for the human.\n10. **When the object tells two stories about how it runs, that disagreement *is* the answer.**\n    An object can carry three accounts of its own execution — what the documentation says, what CI exercises, and what the run records recorded — and they need not agree.\n    Judge **A.1 items (must/007, must/008, must/017) on the documented route**, because that is what a reader will follow; judge **T items on the records**, because that is what produced the results.\n    Where the accounts disagree, the verdict is at most `partial`, the reason names both accounts, and the disagreement is reported as a finding in its own right: a README promising a container that the `Makefile` never invokes is worse than either an honest README or an honest Makefile.\n    Four pairings and where each lands: documentation against the mechanism → **must/007, must/008, must/017**, and **should/002**, because a workflow tested against instructions nobody can follow is not tested; the mechanism against the run records → **must/006 and must/008** (what produced the results is not what is offered to reproduce them); CI against the records → **should/006** (the environment being tested is not the one the results came from); and the object's *metadata* against its own layout — a `dataset_description.json` naming a pipeline the history never ran — which has no checklist item and goes in `extra_items`.\n    If naming both accounts will not fit in 250 characters, name the worse one in the reason and put the other in `evidence`; do not drop it.\n\n    **An accurate disclosure is not a disagreement.**\n    This rule exists for accounts that conflict *silently* — a README promising a container the `Makefile` never invokes.\n    An object whose documentation states the difference and says which route produced the results is telling one story in two parts, and the items above stay `met` on it: the test is whether a reader following the documentation would be misled, not whether two routes exist.\n    Two runs in a row marked a disclosed, accurate two-route object `partial` on four items under this rule; that is this rule being over-applied, and the reason to say so here.\n    **The disclosure has to be true.**\n    A comment in a CI workflow claiming it exercises the recorded environment, in a job whose steps never touch it, is not a disclosure: it is the silent conflict with an extra sentence, and it is worse than silence because it tells a reader not to check.\n11. **A right answer reached by the wrong route is still a finding.**\n    When a probe answers an item from a property the item does not ask about, the verdict may happen to be right and the reasoning is still wrong.\n    Record it in the item's `flags` (`mapping-questioned`), say which property the probe actually measured, and keep your own verdict with `source` ending in `+judgement`.\n    That is how a wrong probe-to-item mapping gets fixed instead of being inherited by every later report.\n\n## Mapping to the app\n\n`scripts/encode_response.py` does this mapping; do not hand-roll it.\nIt also enforces the app's constraints: `value ∈ {\"yes\",\"no\",null}`, `reason` a string of at most 250 characters, and every id known to the pinned checklist.\n{\n  \"schema_version\": 1,\n  \"requirements\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"native-capture\",\n      \"description\": \"Verify the pinned local runtime and retained recent hosted checkpoint/session/ref evidence before launch; each actual launch also retains its native preflight.\",\n      \"source\": \"User: establish native capture access before research; retain existing-host evidence without another capacity or model trial.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"annex-preservation\",\n      \"description\": \"Verify retained proof of newly published artifact bytes independently retrieved from the selected annex route, with exact payload and command identities.\",\n      \"source\": \"User: coordinator preserves complete artifacts with annex and verifies independent retrieval.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"scientific-resources\",\n      \"description\": \"Verify all fixed scientific input bytes and the optional prepared common environment manifest, lock and imports.\",\n      \"source\": \"Registered comparison: identical materialized inputs and available resources across conditions.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
